ligibility agent,\n34\t 161  # the group STS and auth service, and AWS service endpoints.\n35\t 162  apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\n36\t 163  kind: NetworkPolicy\n37\t 164  metadata:\n38\t 165    name: clinic-agent-egress\n39\t 166    namespace: clinic-agent\n40\t 167  spec:\n41\t 168>   podSelector: {}\n42\t 169    policyTypes: [\"Egress\", \"Ingress\"]\n43\t 170    ingress:\n44\t 171      - from:\n45\t 172          - namespaceSelector:\n46\t 173              matchLabels: { kubernetes.io/metadata.name: ingress }\n47\t 174        ports:\n48\t 175          - { port: 8080, protocol: TCP }\n49\t 176    egress:\n50\t 177      - to:\n51\t 178          - namespaceSelector:\n52\t 179              matchLabels: { kubernetes.io/metadata.name: kube-system }\n53\t 180            podSelector:\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\t## B08.2\n57\t\n58\t**Check:** No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.\n59\t\n60\t**How to verify:** 1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants.\n61\t\n62\t**Reviewer's evidence:** k8s/deployment.yaml:63 — \"hostNetwork: false\"\n63\t\n64\t**Reviewer's note:** No broad egress, host mounts or host namespaces.\n65\t\n66\t`k8s/deployment.yaml` lines 51–75 (cited lines marked >):\n67\t```\n68\t  51    labels: { app: clinic-agent }\n69\t  52  spec:\n70\t  53    replicas: 3\n71\t  54    selector:\n72\t  55      matchLabels: { app: clinic-agent }\